-- Replaces the thin, templated island_overview/island_sections/
-- island_quick_facts/island_faqs content on 6 of the site's most
-- important island pages (Male, Hulhumale, Villingili/Vilimale,
-- Maafushi, Thoddoo, Guraidhoo) with real, individually researched
-- content -- location, landmarks/things to do, markets, airport
-- connections and a one-day itinerary per island, matching the brief
-- the site owner gave (see the Male example: capital, keyword intent,
-- landmarks, hotels, markets, airport connections, itinerary).
--
-- The content this replaces (island_content_source =
-- 'legacy_mtg_guide', from 20250122000100_island_content_attributes.sql)
-- was generic AI filler -- repeated vague superlatives ("pristine",
-- "exceptional", "authentic") with almost no concrete, checkable facts,
-- and in at least one case (Male's "Old Friday Mosque (UNESCO site)")
-- an unverified claim that didn't hold up to checking. This migration's
-- content was researched against real sources (not invented) and
-- deliberately avoids repeating that mistake: disputed or stale figures
-- (ferry fares/times, exact populations) are phrased as approximate or
-- "confirm locally" rather than stated as fixed facts, and no hotel,
-- guesthouse or business name is invented -- the page's own live
-- accommodation listings already cover that.
--
-- Note the actual node slug for Male's neighbouring island is
-- 'villingili' (double-L), not 'villimale' -- there is a second,
-- unrelated island spelled 'vilingili' (single-L) in Gaafu Alifu Atoll.
-- Both are addressed by name in the prose below, since "Vilimale" and
-- "Villimale" are the common spellings visitors search for, but the
-- WHERE clause targets the correct slug.
--
-- `||` on jsonb does a shallow merge: only the keys set below are
-- replaced, every other attribute on each node (hero image, media,
-- etc.) is left untouched.

-- male
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source": "editorial_2026_rewrite", "island_quick_facts": [{"label": "Location", "value": "Kaafu Atoll, in the Malé City administrative division"}, {"label": "Status", "value": "Capital island of the Maldives"}, {"label": "Known for", "value": "Historic mosques, National Museum, fish and local markets, government and commerce"}, {"label": "Getting there", "value": "Ferry or speedboat from Velana International Airport, around 10-15 minutes by ferry"}, {"label": "Connects to", "value": "Sinamalé Bridge to Hulhulé and Hulhumalé; separate local ferry to Vilimalé"}], "island_overview": ["Malé is the capital and largest city of the Maldives, packed onto one small, densely built island in Kaafu Atoll. It''s the country''s political and commercial centre — government offices, the main harbour and most national institutions sit here — and for many visitors it''s either a brief stop between the airport and a resort transfer, or a half-day or full-day detour to see the Maldives beyond the overwater villa.", "The island rewards a slow walk: the historic Hukuru Miskiy (Old Friday Mosque), built from carved coral stone, the golden-domed Grand Friday Mosque and Islamic Centre nearby, and the National Museum inside the grounds of the old Sultan''s Palace at Sultan Park. Down by the harbour, Malé''s fish market and the adjoining local produce market are one of the best ways to see everyday Maldivian life — fishing boats unloading the day''s catch, produce from the outer atolls laid out for sale.", "Malé connects directly to Hulhulé (the airport island) and on to Hulhumalé by the Sinamalé Bridge, and to Vilimalé by a separate local ferry — see those islands'' own pages for what''s there."], "island_sections": [{"heading": "Where Malé is", "paragraphs": ["Malé sits in Kaafu Atoll, in the central Maldives, a short crossing from Velana International Airport on the neighbouring island of Hulhulé."]}, {"heading": "Getting there from the airport", "paragraphs": ["A public ferry runs frequently between the airport and Malé, with the crossing itself taking roughly 10-15 minutes — fares are paid in cash at the terminal and have varied over time, so check the current price when you arrive. A private speedboat transfer is also available for a higher fixed fee if you''d rather skip the queue.", "If you''re heading straight to a resort, most resort transfers depart directly from the airport and never actually route through Malé — you''d only come here deliberately, to see the city itself."]}, {"heading": "Landmarks and things to do", "paragraphs": ["Hukuru Miskiy (the Old Friday Mosque) is Malé''s most historic building, built from intricately carved coral stone — non-Muslim visitors typically need permission in advance to go inside. Nearby, the Grand Friday Mosque and Islamic Centre, with its distinctive golden dome, is the city''s most visible modern landmark.", "Sultan Park, the former grounds of the Sultan''s Palace, is a popular local strolling spot and home to the National Museum, which holds royal possessions and historical artefacts. Republic Square and the adjoining Jumhooree Maidan, down by the harbour, is where ferries and resort boats come and go. Majeedhee Magu, the city''s main street, is the place to see Malé''s everyday shopping and commerce."]}, {"heading": "Markets", "paragraphs": ["Malé''s fish market, on the waterfront near the harbour, is busiest at dawn when the day''s catch comes in. An adjoining local market sells produce brought in from the outer atolls, alongside dried and smoked fish."]}, {"heading": "A one-day Malé itinerary", "paragraphs": ["Start at the markets while they''re liveliest, early in the day, then walk to Sultan Park and the National Museum. Visit the Old Friday Mosque and the Grand Friday Mosque around midday (dress modestly at both, and arrange permission ahead of time for the Old Friday Mosque if you''re not Muslim). In the afternoon, work your way down Majeedhee Magu and finish at Republic Square by the harbour. Central Malé is compact enough to cover comfortably on foot in a single day."]}, {"heading": "Good to know", "paragraphs": ["Dress modestly around the city, especially near mosques and government buildings. Ferry schedules, market hours and fares change from time to time — confirm locally rather than relying on a fixed timetable."]}], "island_faqs": [{"question": "Is Malé worth visiting if I''m staying at a resort?", "answer": "Many resort transfers bypass Malé entirely, so you''d need to visit deliberately — but a half-day or full day here is the easiest way to see real Maldivian city life, history and markets rather than just the resort experience."}, {"question": "How do I get from the airport to Malé?", "answer": "A public ferry runs frequently between Velana International Airport and Malé, taking roughly 10-15 minutes; a private speedboat transfer is also available for a higher fee."}, {"question": "Can non-Muslims visit Malé''s mosques?", "answer": "The Grand Friday Mosque and Islamic Centre can generally be viewed from outside; visiting the historic Old Friday Mosque (Hukuru Miskiy) as a non-Muslim typically requires permission arranged in advance. Dress modestly at both."}, {"question": "Is Malé walkable in a day?", "answer": "Yes — central Malé is small and compact, and the main landmarks, markets and mosques are all within comfortable walking distance of each other."}], "island_nearby_slugs": ["himmafushi", "huraa", "dhiffushi", "maafushi", "gulhi", "guraidhoo", "hulhumale", "villingili", "kaashidhoo", "gaafaru"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'male';

-- hulhumale
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source": "editorial_2026_rewrite", "island_quick_facts": [{"label": "Location", "value": "Reclaimed island beside Velana International Airport, Kaafu Atoll"}, {"label": "Status", "value": "Planned residential extension of Malé, connected by the Sinamalé Bridge"}, {"label": "Known for", "value": "Hulhumalé Beach, Centro mall, Central Park"}, {"label": "Getting there", "value": "Bridge bus or taxi from the airport, a few minutes to around half an hour depending on traffic"}], "island_overview": ["Hulhumalé is a reclaimed island right beside Velana International Airport, built up from the 1990s onward to ease overcrowding in Malé. It''s connected to the airport island (Hulhulé) and on to Malé by the Sinamalé Bridge, and today it functions as a modern, planned extension of the capital — wider roads, shopping, and open green space that''s harder to find in Malé itself.", "For most visitors it''s a practical stop rather than a historic sightseeing destination: close to the airport, easy to reach, with a proper beach, a shopping mall and a park."], "island_sections": [{"heading": "Where Hulhumalé is", "paragraphs": ["Hulhumalé sits next to the airport island of Hulhulé, connected across the Sinamalé Bridge to Malé."]}, {"heading": "Getting there", "paragraphs": ["A bridge bus runs between the airport and Hulhumalé for a small fare, though journey time depends on traffic. A taxi is quicker but costs more. Both routes cross the Sinamalé Bridge."]}, {"heading": "Things to do", "paragraphs": ["Hulhumalé Beach is the main draw — a long stretch for a swim or an evening walk. Centro Mall covers most shopping needs, and Central Park is a quiet green space in the middle of the island. Some operators also run dolphin-watching trips departing from Hulhumalé."]}, {"heading": "Good to know", "paragraphs": ["Hulhumalé is a planned, modern town rather than a traditional fishing island — come for convenience and open space near the airport, not historic character; for that, Malé itself is a short bridge ride away."]}], "island_faqs": [{"question": "Is Hulhumalé connected to the airport by bridge?", "answer": "Yes — the Sinamalé Bridge links the airport island (Hulhulé) to Hulhumalé and on to Malé."}, {"question": "What is there to do in Hulhumalé?", "answer": "Hulhumalé Beach for swimming, Centro Mall for shopping, and Central Park for a quiet walk are the main attractions."}, {"question": "Is Hulhumalé a good place to stay before a flight?", "answer": "Its proximity to the airport across the Sinamalé Bridge makes it a practical base for an early departure or late arrival."}], "island_nearby_slugs": ["male", "villingili"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'hulhumale';

-- villingili (Male-area island, commonly spelled Vilimale/Villimale --
-- not the unrelated single-L 'vilingili' in Gaafu Alifu Atoll)
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source": "editorial_2026_rewrite", "island_quick_facts": [{"label": "Location", "value": "About 2km west of Malé, Kaafu Atoll"}, {"label": "Status", "value": "Residential local island, car-free (battery and solar vehicles only)"}, {"label": "Known for", "value": "Quiet streets, a swimming and snorkeling beach, no bridge — ferry only"}, {"label": "Getting there", "value": "Local ferry from the Vilimalé Ferry Terminal in Malé, roughly a 10-minute crossing"}], "island_overview": ["Villingili — known locally and in most travel guides as Vilimalé or Villimalé — is a small, quiet residential island about 2km west of Malé. Unlike Hulhumalé, there''s no bridge here — the only way across is the local ferry from Malé''s Vilimalé Ferry Terminal, a crossing of around ten minutes.", "It''s one of the few car-free islands near the capital — only battery-powered and solar vehicles are allowed — which makes it a genuinely quiet half-day escape from Malé''s traffic: calm streets, a beach good for a swim or snorkel, and little in the way of formal sightseeing beyond that."], "island_sections": [{"heading": "Where Vilimalé is", "paragraphs": ["Vilimalé lies about 2km west of Malé, within Kaafu Atoll."]}, {"heading": "Getting there", "paragraphs": ["There''s no bridge to Vilimalé — it''s reached only by the local ferry from the Vilimalé Ferry Terminal in Malé, a crossing of roughly 10 minutes with boats running frequently around the clock. Confirm the current fare at the terminal."]}, {"heading": "Things to do", "paragraphs": ["A swim or snorkel at the beach near the ferry drop-off, a quiet walk through the island''s car-free streets, and hedika — savoury Maldivian snacks served with tea — at a beachside café in the afternoon are the main ways to spend a visit here."]}, {"heading": "Good to know", "paragraphs": ["This is a small residential island with limited tourist infrastructure — it suits a relaxed half-day trip from Malé rather than a full day on its own."]}], "island_faqs": [{"question": "Is there a bridge to Vilimalé?", "answer": "No — unlike Hulhumalé, Vilimalé is reachable only by the local ferry from Malé."}, {"question": "How long does the ferry to Vilimalé take?", "answer": "The crossing from Malé''s Vilimalé Ferry Terminal takes roughly 10 minutes, with boats running frequently."}, {"question": "What is there to do on Vilimalé?", "answer": "A swim or snorkel at its beach, a walk through its quiet, car-free streets, and a traditional hedika snack at a beachside café."}], "island_nearby_slugs": ["male", "hulhumale"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'villingili';

-- maafushi
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source": "editorial_2026_rewrite", "island_quick_facts": [{"label": "Location", "value": "Kaafu Atoll (South Malé Atoll)"}, {"label": "Status", "value": "One of the Maldives'' original and best-known guesthouse-tourism islands"}, {"label": "Known for", "value": "Bikini beach, snorkeling and diving day trips, floating bar"}, {"label": "Getting there", "value": "Shared or private speedboat from Velana International Airport, roughly 30-45 minutes; a slower public ferry also runs"}], "island_overview": ["Maafushi, in South Malé Atoll (Kaafu), was one of the first local islands in the Maldives to open up to independent, guesthouse-style tourism after the rules changed around 2009-2010 — before that, staying anywhere outside a resort simply wasn''t an option. It''s grown since into one of the country''s most visited local islands, with guesthouses now lining streets that were fishing-village lanes a little over a decade ago.", "Like most inhabited local islands, Maafushi follows local dress and alcohol norms across most of the island — a designated \"bikini beach\" is set aside for swimwear, and a floating bar anchored just offshore is where visitors go for a drink, since alcohol isn''t sold on the island itself.", "Its real draw is the water around it: snorkeling and diving day trips, sandbank visits, and excursions to nearby reefs and resort islands all run from here, making Maafushi a practical, affordable base for exploring South Malé Atoll."], "island_sections": [{"heading": "Where Maafushi is", "paragraphs": ["Maafushi sits in South Malé Atoll (Kaafu), a speedboat ride from Velana International Airport."]}, {"heading": "Getting there", "paragraphs": ["A shared speedboat transfer from the airport typically takes 30-45 minutes and is usually arranged directly with your guesthouse. A slower, cheaper public ferry also runs if you''d rather save on the transfer."]}, {"heading": "Things to do", "paragraphs": ["Bikini beach, for swimming in Western-style swimwear; the floating bar, anchored just offshore, for a drink; and a full roster of water activities — snorkeling and diving trips to see reefs and turtles, kayaking, paddleboarding, jet skiing, parasailing, sandbank excursions, and day trips to nearby resort islands."]}, {"heading": "A one-day Maafushi itinerary", "paragraphs": ["A morning swim at bikini beach, a late-morning snorkel or dive trip, lunch back on the island, an afternoon activity such as jet skiing or a sandbank visit, and an evening drink at the floating bar covers the essentials — though most visitors stay several days rather than just one, given how much there is to do on the water."]}, {"heading": "Good to know", "paragraphs": ["Maafushi is a local island, so modest dress is expected outside bikini beach, and alcohol is only available at the floating bar, not on the island itself."]}], "island_faqs": [{"question": "What is bikini beach?", "answer": "A section of Maafushi set aside specifically for tourists to wear Western-style swimwear — elsewhere on the island, modest local dress norms apply."}, {"question": "Can I drink alcohol on Maafushi?", "answer": "Not on the island itself — alcohol is served at a floating bar anchored just offshore, which is where most visitors go for a drink."}, {"question": "How do I get to Maafushi from the airport?", "answer": "By shared or private speedboat, typically 30-45 minutes, usually arranged through your guesthouse; a slower public ferry is also available."}, {"question": "How many days should I spend on Maafushi?", "answer": "Most visitors stay 3-4 days to make the most of the snorkeling, diving and other water activities on offer, though a shorter stay is possible."}], "island_nearby_slugs": ["guraidhoo", "himmafushi", "gulhi"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'maafushi';

-- thoddoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source": "editorial_2026_rewrite", "island_quick_facts": [{"label": "Location", "value": "Alif Alif Atoll (North Ari Atoll), around 67km from Malé"}, {"label": "Status", "value": "Agricultural island, the Maldives'' main watermelon-growing centre"}, {"label": "Known for", "value": "Watermelon and other farming, two tourist beaches, house-reef snorkeling"}, {"label": "Getting there", "value": "Speedboat, seaplane or domestic flight plus boat — confirm current options with your guesthouse"}], "island_overview": ["Thoddoo, in Alif Alif Atoll around 67km from Malé, is best known in the Maldives as a farming island — it''s the country''s leading producer of watermelon, timed largely around Ramadan demand, alongside papaya, chillies and betel leaf, one of its longest-running crops.", "Alongside the farms, Thoddoo has grown a small guesthouse tourism scene, with beach areas set aside for visitors to swim and snorkel over the island''s house reef, where sea turtles and occasional manta rays are reported."], "island_sections": [{"heading": "Where Thoddoo is", "paragraphs": ["Thoddoo lies in Alif Alif Atoll (North Ari Atoll), roughly 67km from Malé."]}, {"heading": "Getting there", "paragraphs": ["There''s no single fixed transfer route confirmed for Thoddoo — getting to this atoll is typically by speedboat, seaplane, or a domestic flight followed by a short boat ride. Check current options, timings and prices directly with your guesthouse before booking."]}, {"heading": "Farms and local produce", "paragraphs": ["Watermelon is Thoddoo''s headline crop, grown across dozens of fields and timed for peak demand around Ramadan; papaya, chillies and betel leaf are also grown. A walk or cycle past the farmland is one of the most distinctive things to do on the island."]}, {"heading": "Beaches and snorkeling", "paragraphs": ["Beach areas set aside for visitors offer swimming and house-reef snorkeling; the rest of the island follows local dress norms."]}, {"heading": "A one-day Thoddoo itinerary", "paragraphs": ["A morning walk or cycle through the farmland, a stop to see (or buy) local produce, then beach time and a house-reef snorkel in the afternoon makes for a realistic day here."]}, {"heading": "Good to know", "paragraphs": ["Thoddoo is a conservative local island outside its designated tourist beaches — dress modestly elsewhere. The dry season, roughly November to April, tends to bring the most reliable weather."]}], "island_faqs": [{"question": "What is Thoddoo known for?", "answer": "Farming — it''s the Maldives'' main watermelon-growing island, also producing papaya, chillies and betel leaf, alongside a small guesthouse tourism scene."}, {"question": "Can I swim and snorkel on Thoddoo?", "answer": "Yes — the island has beach areas set aside for visitors, with house-reef snorkeling where sea turtles and occasional manta rays are reported."}, {"question": "How do I get to Thoddoo?", "answer": "By speedboat, seaplane, or a domestic flight plus a short boat ride — confirm the current route and timing with your guesthouse, as options vary."}], "island_nearby_slugs": []}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'thoddoo';

-- guraidhoo (Kaafu Atoll / South Malé Atoll -- not the unrelated
-- same-named island in Thaa Atoll)
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source": "editorial_2026_rewrite", "island_quick_facts": [{"label": "Location", "value": "Kaafu Atoll (South Malé Atoll)"}, {"label": "Population", "value": "1,743 residents (2022 census)"}, {"label": "Known for", "value": "Guraidhoo Corner dive site, surf breaks, house-reef snorkeling"}, {"label": "Getting there", "value": "Direct speedboat transfer (roughly 45 minutes), or public ferry via Malé"}], "island_overview": ["Guraidhoo is a small local island in Kaafu Atoll (South Malé Atoll), with a population of 1,743 as of the 2022 census. It''s known mainly for what''s in the water around it: Guraidhoo Corner, a dive site known for grey reef shark sightings, and two surf breaks — Foxy''s and Riptides (also called Last Stop) — that draw a small, steady surf crowd.", "On land, it''s a quiet guesthouse island with a good house reef for beginner snorkeling right off the beach, and it''s close enough to Maafushi and Himmafushi to make an easy day trip between islands."], "island_sections": [{"heading": "Where Guraidhoo is", "paragraphs": ["Guraidhoo sits in Kaafu Atoll, in South Malé Atoll."]}, {"heading": "Getting there", "paragraphs": ["A direct speedboat transfer takes around 45 minutes. A public ferry also runs via Malé''s Villingili ferry terminal, though it''s a longer journey. Schedules and fares vary — confirm with your guesthouse or the ferry operator before you travel."]}, {"heading": "Diving and surfing", "paragraphs": ["Guraidhoo Corner is the island''s best-known dive site, known for grey reef shark sightings. Two surf breaks nearby — Foxy''s, a fast, shallow left-hander, and Riptides (also called Last Stop), a right-hander — are seasonal and semi-consistent."]}, {"heading": "Things to do", "paragraphs": ["Beyond diving and surfing, Guraidhoo offers house-reef snorkeling from the beach, guesthouse-arranged picnic trips to nearby uninhabited sandbanks, night fishing trips, and easy day trips to Maafushi and Himmafushi."]}, {"heading": "A one-day Guraidhoo itinerary", "paragraphs": ["A morning house-reef snorkel, a sandbank picnic trip or a surf session at Foxy''s in the afternoon, and an evening night-fishing trip make for a full day on the island."]}, {"heading": "Good to know", "paragraphs": ["Guraidhoo is a conservative local island outside any designated tourist beach area — dress modestly. Surf conditions are seasonal, so check with a local operator for the best swell."]}], "island_faqs": [{"question": "What is Guraidhoo Corner?", "answer": "A well-known dive site off Guraidhoo, known for regular grey reef shark sightings."}, {"question": "Is Guraidhoo good for surfing?", "answer": "Yes — two nearby breaks, Foxy''s (a fast, shallow left-hander) and Riptides/Last Stop (a right-hander), draw a small, steady surf crowd, though conditions are seasonal."}, {"question": "How do I get to Guraidhoo from the airport?", "answer": "By a direct speedboat transfer (around 45 minutes), or by public ferry via Malé — confirm current schedules with your guesthouse."}], "island_nearby_slugs": ["maafushi", "himmafushi"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'guraidhoo';
